<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">3 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">7 events that happened on 3 May, from 1469 to 1937.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">World Press Freedom Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Proclaimed by the UN General Assembly in 1993 on the anniversary of the Windhoek Declaration, World Press Freedom Day affirms that a free press is essential to democracy and evaluates the state of press freedom worldwide.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1469</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Niccolò Machiavelli is born in Florence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Machiavelli, diplomat of the Florentine republic and author of The Prince, was born. His unsentimental analysis of power made him a founder of modern political thought.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1494</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Columbus sights Jamaica on his second voyage</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Christopher Columbus reached Jamaica, which the Taíno called Xaymaca, and named it Santiago. The island remained under Spanish rule until England captured it in 1655.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1660</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Oliva makes peace between Sweden and Poland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Signed near Danzig, the treaty ended the war between Sweden and Poland-Lithuania. Poland's king renounced his claim to the Swedish throne and recognised Swedish rule in Livonia.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1791</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Poland-Lithuania adopts the Constitution of 3 May</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Sejm passed Europe's first modern codified national constitution, strengthening the monarchy and ending the liberum veto. Russia and Prussia responded with war and the second partition.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1814</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon arrives off Elba to begin his exile</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">HMS Undaunted brought the deposed emperor to Portoferraio, where he landed the next morning to rule his tiny island principality. He escaped in February 1815.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1921</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Northern Ireland comes into being</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Government of Ireland Act took effect, partitioning Ireland and creating Northern Ireland from six north-eastern counties with its own parliament. Partition remains in place today.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1937</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Margaret Mitchell wins the Pulitzer for Gone with the Wind</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Margaret Mitchell's novel of the Civil War and Reconstruction won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction. A huge bestseller, it was filmed in 1939, though its portrayal of slavery is now widely criticised.</p></li></ol>
